            <ArticleTitle>[Ultrasonographic diagnosis of a biliary-digestive fistula with gallstone ileus].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>287-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1055/s-0029-1244849</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="HISTORY AND CLINICAL FINDINGS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 72-year-old woman was admitted to the hospital with upper abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and obstipation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INVESTIGATIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Percutaneous ultrasound found aerobilia, a biliary-digestive fistula, and an ileus with gallstones within the small bowel. The findings were confirmed at computed tomography.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="TREATMENT AND COURSE" NlmCategory="METHODS">A gallstone ileus on the basis of a biliary-digestive fistula was diagnosed. The patient was treated by surgery and with antibiotics.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This case report demonstrates that percutaneous ultrasonography should be used as the first imaging procedure in the diagnosis of acute abdominal pain. This procedure makes it possible to detect a gallstone ileus. It is important also to define various intestinal structures accurately.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Georg Thieme Verlag KG Stuttgart, New York.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
